we're going to begin with urgent  warnings from America's top medical  experts that the US could hit 100,000  daily cases of the corona virus without  action nearly 45,000 cases were reported  just yesterday that's according to Johns  Hopkins and that is the second highest  total since the pandemic started about  45 percent of those cases came from just  three states they are Florida Texas and  right here in California each reported  more than six thousand cases yesterday  top health officials told lawmakers  yesterday they're very concerned the  u.s. is heading the wrong way we are now  having 40 plus thousand new cases a day  I would not be surprised if we go up to  a hundred thousand a day if this does  not turn around it is critical that we  all take the personal responsibility to  slow the transmission of COBIT 19 and  embrace the universal use of face  coverings also on Capitol Hill the  Senate passed an extension of the  paycheck protection program hours before  it was set to expire if the House  approves small businesses will have  until August 8 to apply for federal help  doctor ashish is the director of the  Harvard Global Health Institute and he  testified before the Senate on the  pandemic response just yesterday joins  us now dr. Shah good morning dr. Fauci  we heard say we could have a hundred  thousand new cases per day in the  not-too-distant future how do we avoid  that and perhaps the better question is  how do we get people to actually follow  the guidelines that we know can work yes  sir good morning and thank you for  having me on we can't avoid it we don't  want a hundred thousand cases a day and  we can avoid it we know what works we  got to get everybody to where mass we've  got to really think about indoor  gatherings and whether we can afford  them I think largely we can't no bars no  nightclubs no gems and then we've got to  keep going on testing and tracing if we  can do those things we can get there but  to your question of how do we get people  to comply that's leadership that's  leaders coming out with a consistent  message every day about the importance  of exactly those things you mentioned  yesterday in your testimony on Capitol  Hill that you'd like to see a stronger  response short of that is there a way  that state-by-state there can be enough  of a response to actually slow this  thing yeah so you know we have not had  much in the way of a coordinated federal  response and so we have seen sort of 50  responses to the pandemic and some of  them have been very strong I think some  governors have done a fabulous job and  I'd like to see more governors take this  on but at the end of the day it's gonna  be hard to fight the biggest pandemic in  a century if the federal government is  absent so I do think states can do a lot  but a lot be really helpful if the  federal government helped yeah the  trajectory here seems pretty clear there  are certain segments of the population  that no matter how many times someone  like yourself says wear a mask wash your  hands stay distant it's not gonna to  sink in so can we at least say that the  hospitals and that the medical community  is more prepared now than perhaps they  were in April or May yeah I do think  we're more prepared we've gotten better  at taking care of this disease we have  more protective equipment but by no  means is it sort of foolproof and I'm  worried that hospitals are starting to  run out of beds we are starting to run  out of protective equipment so I still  think there's some serious challenges  ahead  we heard the director of the CDC  mentioned that he'd like to see  universal face coverings but face  coverings are not all created equal and  I want to show people some quick video  here the first image that they're going  to see is just a cough no face cover  covering at all you can see the the  germs spreading out almost 12 feet next  up is a bandana face covering you can  see what comes out of your mouth  contained a little bit more third is a  surgical covering surgical grade face  mask and then finally after we see that  one you can see better containment there  and in pole position very interestingly  a stitched covering which seemed to be  working best of all that is just a  something you're it would be made on a  sewing machine at home when you see  those differences there in terms of how  germs are contained what does that tell  you about what we should be wearing  clearly they're not all created equal  yes sir they're not all created equal  and obviously I first of all I think all  of the evidence not just to study but  others suggest that something is better  than nothing so any face covering is  better  than doing nothing at all but yeah  absolutely those surgical masks are  better the sis clothing cloth masks are  better if people can do those and those  are largely accessible now so people can  do those that would certainly be a big  improvement over bandanas and all of it  is better than nothing at all yeah you  mentioned we are 50 states we've had 50  different responses Europe is also a  collection of countries they've had  different responses but when you look at  the continent there they've been able to  bend the curve in ways we haven't what  lessons can we learn from Europe yeah so  there's one lesson I think then one  lesson only in some ways at the end of  the day which is that we got to take  this virus seriously you know Germany  took a different approach than Italy  which took a different approach than  Denmark so countries can take different  approaches and get there but all of them  took it seriously all of them did social  distancing and really aggressive testing  and that to me are key features that all  of us have to do in the United States  yeah last question here I think a lot of  people are waiting on the vaccine that  might return us to normalcy how hopeful  are you the end of the year is still a  good time table  I think end of the year is gonna be  extremely difficult look we may have a  vaccine that we've identified by the end  of the year but we don't just need to  have a vaccine we need to feel  comfortable that it's safe that it's  effective even if we have it identified  producing hundreds of millions of doses  which I know we're getting going on now  but having it ready for people I think  it'll be more like spring of next year  if everything goes well all right dr.  Shi sha thank you very much

